def build(B, D):
    B.h1("INTRODUCTION")
    B.p("Machine-learning models now support decisions in finance, retail, manufacturing, transport and many other fields. Unlike ordinary software, a model does not fail by crashing or throwing an exception. It fails by being wrong: a loan model may reject deserving applicants, a price model may drift away from the market, or a classifier may behave well on average and badly for one group of people. Because the program keeps running, these failures are easy to miss. This project presents **Testrion**, a full-stack web platform that tests a machine-learning model automatically, in the same spirit as software testing, explains every problem it finds, decides whether the model is fit to be released, and repairs the problems that can be repaired.")
    B.h2("1.1 Overview")
    B.p("Traditional software testing is built on deterministic expectations: if input X is given, output Y is expected. A machine-learning model has no such fixed oracle. Its behaviour is learned from data, so its quality depends on the data it saw, on how that data was cleaned, on the algorithm chosen and on the conditions under which it is used. A model can pass its own accuracy check and still crash when a field is missing, produce impossible predictions for unusual inputs, or treat different regions or age groups unequally [1], [4].")
    B.p("Testrion addresses this gap. A user uploads a tabular CSV file (or chooses one of the built-in sample datasets), selects the column to predict and selects a model. The platform detects whether the task is classification or regression, trains the model on a protected training split, and executes up to 24 automated tests grouped into five categories: data quality, model performance, robustness, reliability, and fairness with data-slice testing. The results are combined into a quality score, a grade and a **deployment gate** that approves or blocks the release of the model. When the gate blocks a model, an **Auto-fix** engine repairs the data pipeline, tunes the model and searches for a better algorithm, and then re-runs every test so that the improvement is demonstrated rather than assumed.")
    B.p("The platform is delivered as a React web application with a FastAPI back-end, accounts and per-user data isolation, a live drift-monitoring service, a Streamlit interface, a command-line deployment gate and a GitHub Actions workflow that runs the same checks on every code push. The chapters that follow describe the literature, the method, the models, the implementation, the datasets used and the measured results.")
    B.h2("1.2 Background of the Study")
    B.p("Industrial experience shows that producing a trained model is only a small part of the effort needed to run machine learning reliably. Sculley et al. described the hidden technical debt of machine-learning systems, including entangled data dependencies and the difficulty of monitoring behaviour after deployment [2]. Breck et al. proposed the ML Test Score, a rubric of tests for data, model development, infrastructure and monitoring that a production system should satisfy [1]. Amershi et al. reported that teams building machine-learning applications face data-management, testing and model-quality challenges that differ from those of conventional software engineering [3]. A large survey by Zhang et al. organised the research on machine-learning testing into properties such as correctness, robustness, fairness and interpretability, and into testing components such as data, learning program and framework [4].")
    B.p("Three practical difficulties motivate automation. First, a single hold-out accuracy figure hides many failure modes: class imbalance can make a useless model look accurate, and leaked features can make a model look perfect. Second, the effort of running many checks by hand after every change is high, so checks are skipped. Third, the data seen in operation changes over time, so a model that was acceptable at release can silently degrade [15]. An automated and repeatable test suite, coupled with a clear release decision, addresses all three.")
    B.h2("1.3 Problem Statement")
    B.p("Models are usually judged by one or two summary metrics on a single test set. This does not reveal whether the model crashes on missing values, behaves sensibly on unusual inputs, depends on a feature that will not exist in production, performs much worse for one group than another, or changes its predictions when features are merely rescaled. There is also no standard automatic procedure that turns the discovery of such a problem into a repair. The problem addressed by this project is to design, implement and evaluate a platform that (i) runs a broad and repeatable set of automated tests on any tabular classification or regression model, (ii) converts the results into an explicit, explainable release decision, (iii) automatically repairs the problems that can be repaired and verifies the repair, and (iv) keeps watching the model after release for data drift.")
    B.h2("1.4 Existing System")
    B.p("In a typical student or small-team workflow, a model is trained in a notebook, evaluated with accuracy on a hold-out set and saved to disk. Continuous-integration services are normally used to test the program code, not the behaviour of the trained model. Open-source tools exist for individual parts of the problem, for example data validation, drift reports and fairness metrics, but they are separate libraries with their own interfaces; a team has to learn each of them, combine their outputs and decide by hand whether the model may be released [8].")
    B.p("Robust-engineering practice for software, such as unit tests, regression tests and metamorphic tests, is only partly transferable. Unit tests cannot say whether a prediction is correct, and an accuracy threshold alone cannot say whether the model is stable, fair or safe at the boundaries of its inputs. There is therefore a need for an integrated system that brings these checks together under one workflow, one score and one decision.")
    B.h2("1.5 Objectives")
    B.p("The main objectives of the project are:")
    B.p("**Objective 1: Automated test generation.** Generate and run tests for data quality, prediction performance, robustness (including edge-case, adversarial and metamorphic tests), reliability and fairness without manual effort.")
    B.p("**Objective 2: Deployment decision.** Convert test outcomes into a quality score and a gate that blocks a release when hold-out accuracy (R² for regression) is below 85%, the demographic parity difference reaches 10%, a class is not detected, the data leaks the target or predictions are not reproducible.")
    B.p("**Objective 3: Automatic problem solving.** Diagnose failed tests and apply data repairs, hyper-parameter tuning and model search, then re-run all tests to prove the improvement, without ever using the hold-out test set to choose a model.")
    B.p("**Objective 4: Monitoring and integration.** Log predictions, detect data drift on live inputs, and run the same checks automatically on every code push through a CI workflow.")
    B.p("**Objective 5: A usable, secure platform.** Provide a web interface with accounts, dataset management, reports, a prediction playground, run comparison and model export, together with a measured demonstration of its performance.")
    B.h2("1.6 Scope of the Project")
    B.p("The platform supports tabular classification and regression with twelve scikit-learn models (six classifiers and six regressors) on CSV data. An interactive run is capped at 6,000 rows for responsiveness, and a larger limit can be set for benchmarking. The evaluation uses five built-in datasets and six demonstration CSV files, of which two built-in datasets (Iris and Wine) are real public data and the remaining nine are synthetic datasets generated by the project code. The findings therefore illustrate how the platform behaves; they do not establish how a given real-world model would perform. Deep-learning models, images, text, time-series forecasting and formal differential-privacy guarantees are outside the scope of the current work, and the platform is designed for local, single-organisation use rather than as a hardened public service.")
